WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.390
ED GLAESER: Historically, most of the people who came to cities were far more concerned with

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.300
survival than with urban amenities.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.690
The Irish who came to Boston fleeing the potato famine,

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.089
the Russians who came to New York to escape the pogroms.

00:00:10.089 --> 00:00:13.820
And the African Americans who came to Chicago to escape the Jim Crow south.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.860
They weren't looking for cooler art museums or Mediterranean climates or

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.220
really good cappuccino.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.570
They wanted safety and a decent living.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:24.260
Most urban migrants in the developing world want the same things today.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.800
But in the wealthy world today,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.160
urban success is increasingly driven by consumption, not production.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.100
I don't mean that cities don't need jobs,

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.170
every successful city needs a robust economic engine.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.680
But I do mean that wealthier,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.660
better educated people are increasingly willing to base their moving decisions

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.065
based on quality of life, even if it means taking a pay cut.

00:00:44.065 --> 00:00:46.595
Los Angeles and Miami are consumer cities, and

00:00:46.595 --> 00:00:49.915
climate provided these places with their original appeal.

00:00:49.915 --> 00:00:53.085
But people also seem to be willing to accept lower real wages to experience

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:54.835
the excitement of New York.

00:00:54.835 --> 00:00:59.015
London's sky high real estate prices are also a sign of its consumer appeal.

00:00:59.015 --> 00:01:02.885
I have often argued that the best economic development strategy is to attract and

00:01:02.885 --> 00:01:05.760
train smart people and then get out of their way.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.700
That may be too simplistic a view, but it does emphasize that improving a city's

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.030
quality of life is actually an economic development strategy.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:16.937
Moreover, since quality of life increases property values, cities seeking more

00:01:16.937 --> 00:01:20.130
revenue have an added incentive to improve their urban amenities.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:23.610
But what attributes make cities particularly attractive places to live?

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.060
Climate is clearly important.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.103
January temperature is the single best predictor of metropolitan area

00:01:28.103 --> 00:01:29.987
growth during the 20th century in the US.

00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:32.370
But city governments can't do much about the weather.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.260
They can do something about the man-made aspects of urban life,

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.180
but what should they focus on?

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.650
Are people look for fun, hip eateries, cool clubs, art galleries?

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.960
Or are they seeking more basic amenities like safe streets and short commutes?

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.770
Should cities be deregulating food trucks and bars, or

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.770
should they be regulating buildings to preserve historic neighborhoods?

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:52.122
In this section, we're going to ask why amenities have become a more important

00:01:52.122 --> 00:01:53.130
part of urban success.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.390
And what cities can do to enhance those amenities.

